> Section 11: MECHANIC’S LIEN WARNING
> Anyone who helps improve Customer property, but who is not paid, may record what is called a mechanics lien
> on Customer property. A mechanics lien is a claim, like a mortgage or home equity loan, made against
> Customer property and recorded with the county recorder. Even if Customer pay Customer contractor in full,
> unpaid subcontractors, suppliers, and laborers who helped to improve Customer property may record mechanics
> liens and sue Customer in court to foreclose the lien. If a court finds the lien is valid, Customer could be forced
> to pay twice or have a court officer sell Customer home to pay the lien. Liens can also affect Customer credit.
> To preserve their right to record a lien, each subcontractor and material supplier must provide Customer with a
> document called a ‘Preliminary Notice.’ This notice is not a lien. The purpose of the notice is to let Customer
> know that the person who sends Customer the notice has the right to record a lien on Customer property if they
> are not paid.
> BE CAREFUL . The Preliminary Notice can be sent up to 20 days after the subcontractor starts work or the
> supplier provides material. This can be a big problem if Customer pay Customer contractor before Customer
> have received the Preliminary Notices.
> Customer will not get Preliminary Notices from Customer prime contractor or from laborers who work on
> Customer project. The law assumes that Customer already know they are improving Customer property.
> PROTECT YOURSELF FROM LIENS . Customer can protect yourself from liens by getting a list from
> Customer contractor of all the subcontractors and material suppliers that work on Customer project. Find out
> from Customer contractor when these subcontractors started work and when these suppliers delivered goods or
> materials. Then wait 20 days, paying attention to the Preliminary Notices Customer receive. PAY WITH JOINT
> CHECKS. One way to protect yourself is to pay with a joint check. When Customer contractor tells Customer it
> is time to pay for the work of a subcontractor or supplier who has provided Customer with a Preliminary Notice,
> write a joint check payable to both the contractor and the subcontractor or material supplier.
> REMEMBER, IF CUSTOMER DO NOTHING, CUSTOMER RISK HAVING A LIEN PLACED ON
> CUSTOMER HOME . This can mean that Customer may have to pay twice, or face the forced sale of
> Customer home to pay what Customer owe.
