> *This is general information, not legal advice. Because the property appears to be in Philadelphia, a Pennsylvania construction/consumer lawyer should review the contract and project record before you terminate or hire a replacement—especially if substantial equipment is on your roof or you have a solar loan.*
> 
> ## Short answer
> 
> **Yes.** The phrase “estimated completion … within six (6) months” is not as strong as a fixed contractual deadline, but it is important evidence of the parties’ intended schedule. Even where a contract lacks a hard completion date, the contractor ordinarily must perform within a **reasonable time**. More than a year after signing—and roughly seven months after its own six-month estimate expired—can support a claim of **material breach**, particularly if GHS has made little progress, has not identified a legitimate external obstacle, or will not give and meet a concrete completion schedule.
> 
> You generally should **not simply declare the contract canceled today**. The prudent path is to send a formal **notice of breach and demand to cure**, giving a short, objectively defined opportunity to produce a binding plan and promptly resume/finish work. If it does not cure, you would have a much stronger basis to terminate for *its* breach, seek repayment/damages, and engage another contractor.
> 
> A court or arbitrator is more likely to award money damages or permit termination than literally compel GHS to install your system. For a delayed solar project, “forcing them to do the work” usually means using a lawyer, formal demand, regulatory complaints, arbitration/litigation, and the credible prospect of termination and damages to obtain performance.
> 
> ## What your contract says
> 
> ### The completion commitment
> 
> Section 4(b) says:
> 
> > “GHS estimates completion of Project within six (6) months of the date on this Agreement.”
> 
> Signed July 10–11, 2025, that estimate points to approximately **January 10–11, 2026**. It also says GHS:
> 
> > “shall commence work shortly after this Agreement is fully executed”  
> > and  
> > “shall provide Customer with periodic updates and communicated as to the progress of the work…”
> 
> The word **“estimates”** gives GHS some flexibility; it does not automatically make January 2026 an absolute drop-dead date. But it does not give GHS an unlimited right to delay. The exceptions are also limited:
> 
> - **Weather/natural conditions**: you agreed to “a reasonable amount of time” if weather prevented completion (§4(c)).
> - **Force majeure**: the contractor must give written notice; if it lasts more than 30 days and materially affects performance, the other party may terminate (§12).
> - **Customer-caused access, permits, HOA, utility, financing, or site-condition issues**: the contract gives GHS some grounds for schedule adjustments, but it should be able to identify the issue, its dates, and what it has done to resolve it.
> 
> A vague explanation such as “scheduling,” “backlog,” or “we are waiting” for over a year is not necessarily a sufficient excuse—particularly if the work could have proceeded or if GHS failed to manage permitting, material ordering, or subcontractors as the contract says it would.
> 
> ### No contractor-default procedure
> 
> Sections 14–15 lay out defaults and remedies **only for the customer**. They do not give GHS a contractual right to delay indefinitely, and they do not eliminate ordinary legal remedies for GHS’s breach. They do mean that you should create a clean written record yourself: identify the breach, demand cure, set a reasonable deadline, and reserve your rights.
> 
> Section 7(m), “Time is of the essence,” expressly applies to **your** obligations. That asymmetry weakens an argument that the six-month estimate was automatically a strict deadline. It does **not** eliminate GHS’s obligation to perform in a reasonable time.
> 
> ### Do not pay for uncompleted work or undelivered materials
> 
> The contract itself prominently states:
> 
> > “IT IS AGAINST THE LAW FOR A CONTRACTOR TO COLLECT PAYMENT FOR WORK NOT YET COMPLETED, OR FOR MATERIALS NOT YET DELIVERED.”
> 
> Its payment schedule ties payments to milestones:
> 
> | Payment | Amount | Listed milestone |
> |---|---:|---|
> | Down payment | $1,000 | Due immediately |
> | First | $7,950 | Site inspection / plans / engineering |
> | Second | $5,565 | Materials delivery |
> | Third | $2,385 | Start of installation |
> | Fourth | $0 | N/A |
> | Final | $2,000 | AHJ final inspection |
> 
> Do not authorize or make another payment merely because GHS asks. Require proof that the corresponding milestone was actually completed. If equipment was purportedly delivered, document exactly what was delivered, where it is, its serial numbers, whether it is undamaged, and whether it is actually allocated to your project.
> 
> ## Recommended next steps
> 
> ### 1. Build a factual project file now
> 
> Create a one-page timeline with:
> 
> - contract date and payments, with proof;
> - site visit, plans, permit application/approval, deliveries, installation dates, inspections, and utility/PTO status;
> - every GHS promise of a date and whether it was met;
> - every request you made for updates;
> - photos of the roof, electrical work, equipment, and any damage;
> - names/contact information for the installer/electrician and relevant subcontractors;
> - all notices from suppliers or subcontractors that could implicate lien rights.
> 
> Also preserve texts, emails, voicemail recordings, proposals, and portal screenshots as PDFs.
> 
> ### 2. Request objective status documents
> 
> Before—or in the same letter as—your breach notice, require GHS to supply:
> 
> 1. The current permit status, permit number, and copies of applications, comments, approvals, and inspection records.
> 2. The interconnection/PTO status and all utility correspondence.
> 3. A complete list of all subcontractors and suppliers, amounts due, and copies of preliminary notices/lien notices, if any.
> 4. Itemized accounting of every payment you made and the work/materials to which it was applied.
> 5. Proof that every person performing electrical work is properly licensed for the jurisdiction where the house is located. Your contract unusually states that labor must be performed by an electrician licensed in **Minnesota**, despite the property appearing to be in Philadelphia. At minimum, insist on the locally required license and permit credentials.
> 6. A detailed completion schedule with actual dates: remaining tasks, responsible party, inspections, correction items, and PTO steps.
> 7. Written confirmation that GHS will not seek or accept payment before the relevant contractual milestone is completed and will provide lien releases/waivers as required.
> 
> ### 3. Send a formal notice to cure / demand for adequate assurance
> 
> Send it by **certified mail, return receipt requested**, to the contractual notice address and to the company’s current principal business address. Also send it by email to the project manager, support address, and any executive/contact you have. Keep the delivery proof.
> 
> Give a deadline that is firm but reasonable—commonly **10 business days** to provide the documents and a definite schedule, plus a short period to resume meaningful work. Do not make the deadline unrealistic if there is an identified permit or utility issue outside GHS’s direct control; instead require evidence and a viable plan.
> 
> A usable starting draft:
> 
> > **Re: Notice of contractor breach; demand to cure and provide adequate assurance — Solar contract dated July 10, 2025, [property address]**
> >
> > Green Home Systems agreed to commence work shortly after execution and payment of the down payment and estimated completion within six months of the agreement date. The estimated completion date was approximately January 10, 2026. The project remains incomplete as of [date], more than [X] months after that date.
> >
> > GHS has not completed the project within the estimated period or a reasonable time and has not provided an adequate, reliable completion plan or periodic progress information. This letter is formal notice that GHS is in breach, or is at minimum in material default for failure to timely perform and provide the contractually required progress communications.
> >
> > To cure, no later than **[date—10 business days after receipt]**, GHS must provide in writing:
> >
> > 1. An itemized status of all contract work, materials, payments, permits, inspections, utility/interconnection steps, and remaining work;
> > 2. Copies of all permit, inspection, utility, and subcontractor/supplier documentation relevant to this project;
> > 3. The identity and applicable license information of the electrician(s) and installer(s) who will perform the remaining work;
> > 4. A binding, date-specific schedule to complete all remaining installation, inspection, and interconnection-assistance obligations, including work beginning no later than **[date]** and physical completion no later than **[date]**; and
> > 5. Written confirmation that no additional payment will be requested except in accordance with the contract’s completed-work/delivered-material payment milestones, together with appropriate lien releases/waivers.
> >
> > If GHS does not timely cure, I will treat its failure as a material breach and pursue all available remedies, including termination for contractor default, recovery of payments and completion/delay damages, and engagement of a replacement contractor. Nothing in this notice waives any right or remedy. I expressly reserve all rights.
> >
> > Please direct all substantive communications regarding this notice in writing.
> 
> Use only dates you are prepared to enforce. If you are already sure you no longer trust them to finish, an attorney can draft a more direct notice making clear that a failure to cure will result in termination. That helps avoid GHS later characterizing your departure as a voluntary “cancellation.”
> 
> ### 4. Do not sign a “completion certificate” or loan disbursement authorization
> 
> The contract tries to make the remaining balance due at “Completion,” defined mainly as final AHJ inspection approval—not PTO. But it also says you should not withhold authorization for utility/interconnection delays after completion. That clause does **not** require you to certify completion when the installation or inspection is not actually complete.
> 
> If there is third-party financing, promptly check its dispute process. A solar lender is separate from GHS under this contract. **Do not unilaterally stop loan payments** or assume that a dispute with GHS automatically pauses the loan; give the lender written notice of the dispute and ask what formal dispute/hold procedures apply.
> 
> ### 5. Protect against liens and double payment
> 
> The contract’s lien warnings are generic and cite California law, which may not match the law where your home is located. But the practical concern is real: an unpaid installer or supplier can sometimes assert a lien even when you paid the prime contractor.
> 
> Before paying anything further:
> 
> - obtain an itemized list of suppliers/subcontractors and payment status;
> - seek **conditional lien waivers** before payment and **unconditional waivers** after funds clear, to the extent local law recognizes them;
> - consider joint checks only after legal advice and confirmation of what is actually owed;
> - do not assume a GHS promise that it paid a subcontractor is enough.
> 
> ### 6. Get local legal and regulatory leverage
> 
> The contract is conspicuously mismatched to the apparent project location: it refers to a Minnesota electrician, California Civil Code lien provisions, California law, and Los Angeles venue. It also contains an apparent conflict between a Los Angeles court venue clause (§18) and a statement about FAA arbitration on page 17, without a clearly drafted arbitration procedure in the text you provided.
> 
> That does **not** mean the whole contract is void. It does mean a local consumer/construction attorney should assess:
> 
> - whether the California choice-of-law / Los Angeles venue provision can be enforced against a homeowner with an out-of-state residential project;
> - whether Pennsylvania home-improvement or Philadelphia contractor-licensing/consumer-protection requirements apply and whether the contract complied;
> - whether the purported arbitration provision is actually enforceable;
> - whether GHS and the actual installer had the licenses and registrations required where the work occurred;
> - whether you can seek attorney’s fees, statutory remedies, rescission, or consumer-protection remedies in addition to ordinary breach damages.
> 
> Useful escalation targets typically include the relevant **local building/permitting department**, **state contractor/licensing authority**, and **state attorney general consumer-protection office**. A lawyer can identify the correct agencies based on the actual property jurisdiction and the contractor/installer entities.
> 
> ## About GHS’s “cancellation fees”
> 
> Section 16 lists $1,000 for inspection, $2,500 for plans/engineering, $2,000 for permitting, and $3,000 for restocking. GHS may invoke these if you simply say “I cancel.”
> 
> That is why the distinction matters:
> 
> - **Voluntary cancellation for convenience:** GHS will argue Section 16 applies.
> - **Termination after GHS materially breaches and fails to cure:** you are not canceling because you changed your mind; you are ending the contract because the contractor failed to perform. GHS may still dispute it, but its fee clause is much less decisive.
> 
> Also, a pre-set fee is not automatically collectible just because it is printed in a contract. Its enforceability depends on applicable law, whether it reasonably reflects actual losses, what work/materials were actually provided, and other facts. Do not concede these charges in writing.
> 
> ## Bottom line
> 
> Your strongest immediate move is:
> 
> 1. **Document the delay and current status.**
> 2. **Send a certified written notice to cure with a 10-business-day document/schedule deadline and concrete completion milestones.**
> 3. **Make no unearned payment and preserve lien protections.**
> 4. **If GHS does not provide credible assurance and promptly perform, consult local counsel to issue a termination/demand letter and pursue replacement, refund, and damages.**
> 
> At more than a year after contracting, with a six-month estimated completion date and no clear contractual excuse, you have a substantial basis to insist that GHS either promptly perform on a real schedule or face termination and legal remedies.
